39	- `T` has a parameter `X` bounded by `T` itself, as `trait T[\X extends T[\X\]\]` (P-146), or by a self-typed trait `U[\X\]` that `T` extends, as `trait T[\X extends U[\X\]\] extends U[\X\]`.
40	
41	In `T`'s bodies, `self` has the intersection type of `T[\X\]` and `X`. So `floor(self): I = self` in `Integral[\I extends Integral[\I\]\]` is valid without a cast. The checker refuses a type that extends `T[\A\]` and is not an `A`. A compiled body of `T` that returns `self` at `X` runs, also where `X` is an object type. Three shapes still fail when the class loads:
42	
43	- a function expression or a local function that returns `self` at `X` fails the JVM's verification (row 1050);
44	- a conditional with `self` in one arm and another value in the other fails the JVM's verification (row 1051). `StandardTotalOrder`'s `MIN` and `MAX` have this shape (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:291`);
45	- a default body that calls a method on another argument of type `X` gives a `ClassCircularityError` where the object lists `T` first in its `extends` clause (row 1052).
46	
47	For the first two shapes, bind `self` at the type first, as `r: R = self`, and use `r`. This workaround has been tested only at a type that a `comprises` clause lists, not at `X`.
